{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":39878410,"ns":0,"title":"Los Bronces mine","revisions":[{"revid":1368235550,"parentid":1342809160,"minor":true,"timestamp":"2026-08-07T21:32:03Z","size":5475,"sha1":"66902d222fe6930c2387e279d016ba04f9eb9e02","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{Short description|Copper mine in Chile}}\n{{Infobox Mine\n| name = Los Bronces mine\n| image =Campamento minero.JPG\n| image_size =\n| caption =\n| pushpin_map =\n| pushpin_label =\n| pushpin_caption =\n| coordinates = {{coord|33|08|42|S|70|16|16|W|format=dms|display=inline,title}}\n| display =\n| location = [[Santiago Metropolitan Region]]/[[Valparaíso Region]], [[Chile]]\n| stock_exchange = \n| stock_code = \n| website =\n| date_acquired = 2002\n| products = [[Copper]]\n| financial_year = 2024\n| amount = {{decrease}} 172,400 tonnes of copper<ref name=anuario2024>{{Cite report |title=Anuario de estadisticas del cobre y otros minerales |year=2025 |publisher=[[Chilean Copper Commission]] |trans-title=Yearbook: Copper and Other Mineral Statistics: 2005 2024}}</ref>\n| date_opened = \n| date_closed = \n| owner = [[Anglo American Sur]]\n}}\nThe '''Los Bronces mine''' is a large [[mining|copper mine]] located in central [[Chile]], on the border of the [[Santiago Metropolitan Region]] and the [[Valparaíso Region]]. The processing plant is located at 3,300 m above sea level, with mining operations extending up to 4200 m above sea level. Los Bronces represents one of the largest copper reserves in [[Chile]] and in the world having estimated reserves of 3.13 billion tonnes of ore grading 0.32% copper.<ref name=\"Iacobeni\">{{cite web|url=http://www.angloamerican.com/~/media/Files/A/Anglo-American-Plc/media/fact_book/copper_2012|title=Copper mines|year=2011|publisher=angloamerican.com|accessdate=2013-07-06|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130202103617/http://www.angloamerican.com/~/media/Files/A/Anglo-American-Plc/media/fact_book/copper_2012|archive-date=2013-02-02|url-status=dead}}</ref>\n\nThe [[ore concentrate]] produced in the main contained as of 2020 about 4% [[chalcopyrite]], 32% [[chalcocite]] and 45% [[pyrite]].<ref>{{Cite journal |title=Processing of Complex Materials in the Copper Industry: Challenges and Opportunities Ahead |journal=[[JOM (journal)|JOM]] |last=Alvear Flores |first=Gerardo |url=https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11837-020-04255-9.pdf |volume=72 |issue=10 |last2=Risopatrón |first2=Carlos |last3=Pease |first3=Joe |year=2020 |doi=10.1007/s11837-020-04255-9}}</ref>\n\nFollowing the [[Chilean nationalization of copper]] in the early 1970s stateowned company [[ENAMI (Chile)|ENAMI]] came to own the mine which was by then known as Disputada de Las Condes. In late 1977 ENAMI sold the mine to [[Exxon]] for 93 million US$.<ref name=ciperbronce>{{Cite news |title=Las desconocidas operaciones de Anglo que bloquean el acceso de Codelco a la propiedad de Los Bronces |last=Ramos |first=Marcela |date=2011-12-02 |url=https://www.ciperchile.cl/2011/12/02/las-desconocidas-operaciones-de-anglo-que-bloquean-el-acceso-de-codelco-a-la-propiedad-de-los-bronces/ |access-date=2025-06-10 |work=[[Ciper]] |last2=Figueroa |first2=Juan Pablo |language=es}}</ref> Exxon later sold the mine to [[Anglo American plc]] for 1300 million US$ in 2002.<ref name=ciperbronce/><ref>{{Cite news |title=Disputada: De Exxon a Angloamerican |date=2011-10-13 |url=https://www.df.cl/empresas/disputada-de-exxon-a-angloamerican |access-date=2025-12-28 |work=[[Diario Financiero]] |language=es}}</ref> \n\nIn Los Bronces mining folklore tells of man known as \"El Futre\" (lit. \"The Snob\").<ref name=Sonia2015>{{Cite book|title=Mitos de Chile: Enciclopedia de seres, apariciones y encantos|last=Montecino Aguirre|first=Sonia|publisher=[[Catalonia (publisher)|Catalonia]] |year=2015|isbn=978-956-324-375-8 |page=297|chapter=Futre, El |language=es}}</ref> El Futre is said to be a formally dressed man who appear in drifts murdering or scaring workers in order to steal their salary.<ref name=Sonia2015/>  \n\nThe mine holds [[The Copper Mark]] environmental certificate.<ref name=bnamericas>{{Cite web |title=The role of the Copper Mark in responsible mining |url=https://www.bnamericas.com/en/interviews/the-role-of-the-copper-mark-in-responsible-mining |date=2022-09-09 |access-date=2025-08-21 |website=Bnamericas}}</ref> It lies in an area of [[rock glacier]]s.<ref>{{Cite journal |title=Minería y glaciares rocosos: impactos ambientales, antecedentes políticos y legales, y perspectivas futuras |journal=[[Revista de Geografía Norte Grande]] |last=Brenning |first=Alexander |volume=47 |pages=143-158|url=https://www.scielo.cl/pdf/rgeong/n47/art08.pdf |last2=Azócar |first2=Guillermo|year=2010|language=es}}</ref>\n\n== Geology ==\n{{expand section|date=December 2025}}\nMineralization at Los Bronces is thought to be due to its position at the intersection of two large [[fault (geology)|fault systems]]. This favoured the rise of magma and the subsequent circulation of mineral-rich fluids.<ref name=Piqueretal1019>{{cite journal |last1=Piquer Romo |first1=José Meulen |last2=Yáñez |first2=Gonzálo |last3=Rivera |first3=Orlando |last4=Cooke |first4=David |date=2019 |title=Long-lived crustal damage zones associated with fault intersections in the high Andes of Central Chile |url=http://www.andeangeology.cl/index.php/revista1/article/view/V46n2-3106/pdf |journal=[[Andean Geology]] |volume=46 |issue=2 |pages=223–239 |doi=10.5027/andgeoV46n2-3108 |access-date=June 9, 2019|doi-access=free }}</ref>\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:Anglo American Sur]]\n[[Category:Copper mines in Chile]]\n[[Category:Mines in Valparaíso Region]]\n[[Category:Mines in Santiago Metropolitan Region]]\n[[Category:Open-pit mines]]\n[[Category:Surface mines in Chile]]"}}}],"contentmodel":"wikitext","pagelanguage":"en","pagelanguagehtmlcode":"en","pagelanguagedir":"ltr","touched":"2026-11-02T09:49:26Z","lastrevid":1368235550,"length":5475}]}}